<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Cao</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 487 BCE (approx.) · capital Taoqiu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state on the eastern plain founded by a brother of King Wu, best remembered for an insult to a wandering prince that brought down the wrath of Jin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu</a> enfeoffed his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-shu-zhenduo">Zhenduo</a>, known as Cao Shu, at Taoqiu, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingtao-district">Dingtao</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. The site lay in a rich trading region of the eastern plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cao was a modest Ji-clan state that usually followed the leading power of the day. Its most famous episode came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-gong-of-cao">Duke Gong of Cao</a> spied on the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin">Prince Chong'er</a> of Jin in his bath to see his reputedly joined ribs. After Chong'er became Duke Wen of Jin he invaded Cao in 632 BCE and captured its ruler.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Caught between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-song">State of Song</a> and the northern powers, Cao was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> in 487 BCE and its last ruler killed. The surname Cao, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China's</a> most common, is traditionally traced in part to this state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1046 BCE – 487 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.89%;width:11.12%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 487 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taoqiu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cao Shu Zhenduo, Duke Gong of Cao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-766&amp;focus=state-of-cao" aria-label="State of Cao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7608 907 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Cao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7928 1163L7768 1193M7928 1163L7940 1188"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7940 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7928 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7928 1163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-766&amp;focus=state-of-cao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

